---
import BlogLayout from '../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';

const bodyContent = `<p>Your MCP server can query a database, call an API, read a file system. But the agent holding those results cannot send them to another agent without building your own transport layer. This is the networking gap in the MCP ecosystem.</p>

  <p>Pilot Protocol fills that gap. It gives every agent a permanent virtual address, encrypted peer-to-peer tunnels, NAT traversal, and hostname-based discovery. This article walks through the concrete problem and shows you how to connect MCP-equipped agents across any network - with zero networking configuration.</p>

  <section>
  <h2 id="the-problem">The Problem: MCP Stops at the Agent Boundary</h2>

  <p>MCP (Model Context Protocol) standardizes how agents access tools. An agent runs an MCP client, connects to MCP servers, and invokes tools through a structured JSON-RPC interface. This works well for the vertical axis: agent → tool.</p>

  <p>But what about the horizontal axis? Agent A queries a Postgres database via MCP and gets 1,842 rows of sales data. Agent B needs those results to generate a report. How does Agent A send the data to Agent B?</p>

  <p>Without a networking layer, your options are:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>HTTP webhooks</strong> - Agent B needs a public URL. Fails behind NAT. Requires a web server. No encryption by default.</li>
    <li><strong>Message broker</strong> (Kafka, RabbitMQ, Redis) - Now you have infrastructure to manage, configuration to write, and a single point of failure.</li>
    <li><strong>Shared database</strong> - Agent A writes results to a DB, Agent B polls for them. Latency. Complexity. Polling waste.</li>
    <li><strong>Cloud pub/sub</strong> (SNS, Cloud Pub/Sub) - Vendor lock-in. API keys. Monthly bills. Egress charges.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Every one of these adds infrastructure, configuration, and failure modes. Every one requires Agent B to have a reachable endpoint or both agents to share a third-party service.</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>The core issue:</strong> MCP gives agents eyes and hands (access to data and tools). But it gives them no voice - no way to tell another agent what they found. Pilot provides that voice.</p>
  </div>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2 id="the-solution">The Solution: One Install, Direct Connection</h2>

  <p>Pilot Protocol is a daemon and a CLI (static binaries, no runtime dependencies) that give your agent a network identity, encrypted connections, and the ability to dial any other agent by hostname - regardless of NAT, firewall, or cloud provider.</p>

  <p>Here is what the full flow looks like:</p>

  <pre><code># Install Pilot (one command)
curl -fsSL https://pilotprotocol.network/install.sh | sh

# Start Agent A with a hostname (--public lets peers resolve the name)
pilotctl daemon start --hostname analyst-agent --public

# Start Agent B on a different machine
pilotctl daemon start --hostname reporter-agent --public

# One-time trust: each side sends a handshake (crossed requests approve each other)
pilotctl handshake reporter-agent    # on A
pilotctl handshake analyst-agent     # on B

# Agent A sends MCP query results to Agent B (they land in B's inbox)
pilotctl send-message reporter-agent --data '{"rows":1842,"summary":"Q4 up 23%"}' --type json</code></pre>

  <p>That is the entire setup. No YAML. No Docker. No cloud accounts. No port forwarding. Agent A reaches Agent B by hostname; after the one-time mutual trust handshake (both sides send one, or the peer approves the request with <code>pilotctl approve</code>, or runs with <code>-trust-auto-approve</code> in a trusted environment), an encrypted tunnel is established and the data flows between them, directly when NAT allows.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2 id="architecture">Architecture: MCP Vertical + Pilot Horizontal</h2>

  <p>Think of agent infrastructure as two orthogonal planes:</p>

  <p><strong>Vertical (MCP):</strong> Agent reaches down into systems. Queries databases, calls APIs, reads files, executes code. The agent talks to systems.</p>

  <p><strong>Horizontal (Pilot):</strong> Agent reaches across to peers. Discovers other agents, opens encrypted connections, sends messages, delegates tasks. The agent talks to agents.</p>

  <p>Neither layer knows about the other. Your MCP configuration is unchanged. Your Pilot configuration is unchanged. At the application level, your agent code does both:</p>

  <pre><code>// Go example: Agent A - query via MCP, deliver via Pilot
// 1. Query database through MCP
results := mcpClient.CallTool("postgres", "query", map[string]any{
    "sql": "SELECT * FROM sales WHERE quarter = 'Q4'",
})

// 2. Send results to peer agent via Pilot (data exchange, port 1001)
d, _ := driver.Connect("/tmp/pilot.sock")
info, _ := d.ResolveHostname("reporter-agent")
addr, _ := protocol.ParseAddr(info["address"].(string))
cl, _ := dataexchange.Dial(d, addr)
cl.SendJSON(results.JSON()) <span class="comment">// waits for the receiver's ACK</span>
cl.Close()</code></pre>

  <pre><code># Python example: Agent A - query via MCP, deliver via Pilot
import pilotprotocol as pilot
import mcp

# 1. Query database through MCP
async with mcp.Client("postgres-server") as client:
    results = await client.call_tool("query", sql="SELECT * FROM sales")

# 2. Send results to peer agent via Pilot (lands in its inbox)
with pilot.Driver() as d:
    d.send_message("reporter-agent", results.to_json().encode(), msg_type="json")</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2 id="crossing-nats">Crossing NATs: The Real Problem</h2>

  <p>The reason you cannot just "open a TCP connection" between two agents is NAT. <a href="/blog/connect-ai-agents-behind-nat-without-vpn">Most networks involve NAT</a>. Your MCP server is behind a firewall. Your peer agent is behind a different firewall. Neither has a public IP.</p>

  <p>Pilot handles this transparently with three-tier traversal:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>STUN discovery</strong> - Determines your public-facing endpoint.</li>
    <li><strong>UDP hole-punching</strong> - For restricted and port-restricted cone NAT, the beacon coordinates simultaneous punches from both sides.</li>
    <li><strong>Relay fallback</strong> - For symmetric NAT (unpredictable port mapping), data is relayed through the beacon. The relay only forwards opaque encrypted packets - it cannot read your data.</li>
  </ol>

  <p>All of this is automatic. Your code dials a hostname. Pilot tries direct, hole-punched and relayed paths in turn.</p>

  <pre><code># This works whether the peer is on the same LAN, behind NAT, or across clouds
pilotctl send-message reporter-agent --data '{"data": "..."}'
# prints the receiver's acknowledgement ("ack": "ACK TEXT 15 bytes")
pilotctl peers --all
# PATH column: direct or relay</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2 id="scenario-walkthrough">Full Scenario: Database Agent → Report Agent</h2>

  <p>Let us walk through the complete flow for the most common MCP + Pilot pattern:</p>

  <p><strong>Agent A (analyst-agent):</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>MCP servers: Postgres, Python executor</li>
    <li>Location: Corporate office, behind NAT</li>
    <li>Job: Query sales data, run analysis</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Agent B (reporter-agent):</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>MCP servers: Slack API, email</li>
    <li>Location: AWS EC2 instance</li>
    <li>Job: Receive insights, post to Slack, send email reports</li>
  </ul>

  <p><strong>Step 1: Install on both machines</strong></p>
  <pre><code># Same command on both machines
curl -fsSL https://pilotprotocol.network/install.sh | sh</code></pre>

  <p><strong>Step 2: Start daemons</strong></p>
  <pre><code># Machine A (behind corporate NAT)
pilotctl daemon start --hostname analyst-agent --public

# Machine B (AWS EC2)
pilotctl daemon start --hostname reporter-agent --public</code></pre>

  <p><strong>Step 3: Establish trust</strong></p>
  <pre><code># Agent A trusts Agent B
pilotctl handshake reporter-agent
# Agent B trusts Agent A
pilotctl handshake analyst-agent</code></pre>

  <p><strong>Step 4: Agent A queries and delivers</strong></p>
  <pre><code># Agent A's application code (mcp_* stand for your MCP client calls)
import pilotprotocol as pilot

results = mcp_query("SELECT * FROM sales WHERE quarter = 'Q4'")
analysis = mcp_run_python(f"analyze({results})")

# Send to reporter - Pilot handles NAT, encryption, everything
with pilot.Driver() as d:
    d.send_message("reporter-agent", analysis.to_json().encode(), msg_type="json")</code></pre>

  <p><strong>Step 5: Agent B receives and acts</strong></p>
  <pre><code># Agent B's application code - messages land in the daemon's inbox
import json, subprocess

def latest_from(peer):
    out = subprocess.run(["pilotctl", "--json", "inbox", "--from", peer, "--latest"],
                         capture_output=True, text=True)
    return json.loads(json.loads(out.stdout)["data"]["messages"][0]["data"])

data = latest_from("analyst-agent")
mcp_call("slack", "post_message", channel="#reports", text=data["summary"])
mcp_call("email", "send", to="cfo@company.com", body=data["full_report"])</code></pre>

  <p>The application data path is: Postgres → Agent A → encrypted Pilot tunnel → Agent B → Slack/Email. The tunnel is direct when NAT permits and uses encrypted beacon relay fallback otherwise; no separate application broker needs to be deployed.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2 id="multi-agent-pipelines">Multi-Agent Pipelines Over Pilot</h2>

  <p>The pattern scales to N agents. Each agent has different MCP tools, and they coordinate over Pilot tunnels:</p>

  <pre><code># Pipeline: scraper → analyzer → reporter
# Each agent has its own MCP servers. send_to() wraps Driver.send_message,
# latest_from() reads the inbox as in Step 5.

# scraper (MCP: browser, fetch)
data = mcp_fetch("https://api.example.com/data")
send_to("analyzer", data)

# analyzer (MCP: postgres, python)
raw = latest_from("scraper")
insights = mcp_run_python(f"analyze({raw})")
send_to("reporter", insights)

# reporter (MCP: slack, email)
results = latest_from("analyzer")
mcp_call("slack", "post_message", text=results["summary"])</code></pre>

  <p>Every link in the pipeline is an encrypted tunnel, direct when NAT allows and relayed otherwise. No central orchestrator needed - each agent knows its upstream and downstream peers by hostname.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2 id="discovery">Discovering MCP Servers Behind NAT</h2>

  <p>A common pattern: you have an MCP tool server (say, a database wrapper) running behind NAT. Other agents need to discover and use it.</p>

  <p>With Pilot, the MCP server machine registers a hostname:</p>

  <pre><code># On the MCP server machine (behind NAT, no public IP)
pilotctl daemon start --hostname db-tool-server</code></pre>

  <p>Any trusted agent can now resolve it:</p>

  <pre><code># On any other machine, anywhere (after trust)
pilotctl find db-tool-server</code></pre>

  <p>To reach the MCP server itself, something on that machine has to listen on a Pilot port and hand connections to it. Pilot doesn't expose local services on its own. For an HTTP-based MCP server, that can be a small Go program that accepts on port 80 with the driver (<code>d.Listen(80)</code>) and proxies each connection to the local endpoint. The MCP server never needs a public IP, a reverse proxy, or an ngrok tunnel: Pilot's hostname resolution and NAT traversal make that Pilot port reachable from any trusted agent.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2 id="vs-existing">How This Differs From MCP + Pilot: Tools and Network</h2>

  <p>We published <a href="/blog/mcp-plus-pilot-tools-and-network">MCP + Pilot: Give Your Agent Tools AND a Network</a> earlier, which covers the conceptual architecture: what each protocol provides and how they complement each other.</p>

  <p>This article focuses on the practical networking problem:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>NAT traversal</strong> - Concrete walkthrough of how agents behind different firewalls connect</li>
    <li><strong>Zero configuration</strong> - Emphasis on one-command install, no infrastructure to manage</li>
    <li><strong>Code examples</strong> - Go and Python sketches (the MCP calls stand in for your own client code)</li>
    <li><strong>Full scenario</strong> - Step-by-step from install to data flowing between agents</li>
  </ul>

  <p>If you want the "why" - read the original post. If you want the "how" - you are in the right place.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2 id="get-started">Get Started</h2>

  <pre><code># Install
curl -fsSL https://pilotprotocol.network/install.sh | sh

# Or with Python
pip install pilotprotocol</code></pre>

  <p>That is it. One command. Your MCP-equipped agent now has a network.</p>

  <p><strong><a href="/for/mcp">Give your MCP servers a network →</a></strong></p>
  </section>`;
